INSERT INTO questions (role_id, topic, difficulty, question_text, ideal_answer) VALUES
(2, 'Semantic HTML', 'Easy', 'What is semantic HTML and why is it important for web development?', 'Semantic HTML refers to the use of markup tags that clearly describe their meaning to both the browser and the developer, such as header, footer, article, and section instead of generic divs. This practice is crucial because it improves accessibility for screen readers and assistive technologies, allowing users with disabilities to navigate web pages more effectively. Additionally, semantic markup enhances search engine optimization by providing clear structural hints to web crawlers about the relative importance and context of different content sections, leading to better discoverability and indexing.'),
(2, 'CSS Selectors', 'Easy', 'Explain how CSS selectors work and describe the difference between class selectors and ID selectors.', 'CSS selectors are patterns used to target and select specific HTML elements on a web page so that styling rules can be applied to them. Class selectors, denoted by a dot prefix, are designed to be reusable across multiple elements throughout a document, allowing developers to apply consistent styling to groups of similar components. In contrast, ID selectors, denoted by a hash prefix, must be uniquely assigned to a single element per page, giving them higher specificity and making them ideal for targeting unique structural layout components or specific functional hooks.'),
(2, 'JavaScript Variables', 'Easy', 'What are the differences between var, let, and const when declaring variables in JavaScript?', 'The keywords var, let, and const are used to declare variables in JavaScript, each with distinct scoping and reassignment rules. Variables declared with var are function scoped and hoisted to the top of their execution context, which can lead to unexpected bugs due to variable leakage. In contrast, let and const are block scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the nearest enclosing set of curly braces. Furthermore, while let allows variable reassignment, const enforces immutability on the variable binding once initialized, promoting safer and more predictable code.'),
(2, 'DOM Manipulation', 'Easy', 'What is the Document Object Model and how do JavaScript methods interact with it?', 'The Document Object Model, commonly known as the DOM, is a programming interface that represents web documents as a hierarchical tree structure where every HTML element is an object node. JavaScript interacts with this tree by utilizing built-in methods and properties to dynamically access, modify, create, or delete elements and their attributes in real time. This capability allows developers to build highly interactive and responsive web applications that update user interfaces instantaneously in response to user actions without requiring full page reloads.'),
(2, 'CSS Box Model', 'Medium', 'Describe the components of the CSS box model and explain how they affect element sizing.', 'The CSS box model is a foundational layout concept that dictates how every HTML element is rendered as a rectangular box comprising four distinct concentric layers. These layers include the content area at the center, surrounded successively by padding, borders, and margins, which together determine the total spatial footprint of the element on the page. Understanding this model is essential because standard box sizing calculates total width by adding content, padding, and borders together, meaning developers must account for these additive dimensions to prevent layout overflow and maintain precise alignment across different viewports.'),
(2, 'Flexbox Layout', 'Medium', 'Explain how the CSS Flexbox layout model simplifies alignment and distribution of space among items.', 'The CSS Flexbox layout model provides a powerful and efficient one-dimensional mechanism for arranging items along a single axis, whether horizontally or vertically, within a container. By turning on flex formatting, containers gain the ability to automatically distribute available space and dynamically adjust the dimensions of child items to fit varying screen sizes seamlessly. Key properties like justify content and align items give developers precise control over main-axis and cross-axis alignment, eliminating the need for complex float hacks and making responsive UI design significantly more intuitive and maintainable.'),
(2, 'Event Bubbling', 'Medium', 'What is event bubbling in the JavaScript DOM event model and how does it function?', 'Event bubbling is a phase in the propagation of DOM events where an event triggered on a nested child element propagates upward through its parent ancestors all the way to the document root. When an event occurs, the browser first executes listeners on the target element during the capture phase, and then allows the event to bubble up, triggering any matching event listeners attached to ancestor containers. This mechanism enables event delegation, allowing developers to attach a single listener to a common parent element to efficiently manage events across numerous dynamic child nodes.'),
(2, 'Fetch API', 'Medium', 'How does the JavaScript Fetch API facilitate asynchronous network requests and data handling?', 'The Fetch API provides a modern, promise-based interface for making asynchronous HTTP network requests to fetch resources from servers across the web. Unlike older callback-based methods, fetch returns a Promise that resolves to the Response object representing the completion of the network call, which can then be parsed into usable formats like JavaScript Object Notation. By leveraging asynchronous programming constructs like async and await, developers can write clean, readable code to handle server responses, manage error states, and update user interfaces smoothly as data arrives.'),
(2, 'Responsive Design', 'Hard', 'Explain the core principles of responsive web design and how media queries enable cross-device adaptability.', 'Responsive web design is an approach that aims to craft web pages to render well on a variety of devices and window or screen sizes, ensuring optimal usability across desktops, tablets, and smartphones. The core principles rely on fluid grid layouts, flexible images, and CSS media queries that inspect device characteristics such as width, height, resolution, and orientation. Media queries allow developers to apply conditional styling rules breakpoints dynamically, enabling layouts to rearrange, resize, or hide elements seamlessly as the viewport dimensions change, delivering an uncompromised user experience everywhere.'),
(2, 'CSS Positioning', 'Hard', 'Compare and contrast the different CSS positioning schemes and explain how stacking contexts are formed.', 'CSS positioning schemes determine how elements are placed within a document layout through properties like static, relative, absolute, fixed, and sticky. Static positioning is the default document flow, while relative positioning offsets an element from its normal position without altering surrounding layout space. Absolute positioning removes an element from the normal flow entirely, positioning it relative to its closest positioned ancestor, whereas fixed positioning anchors elements relative to the browser viewport. Stacking contexts are formed by elements with specific positioning and z-index values, determining the exact rendering order of overlapping visual layers on the screen.');
INSERT INTO questions (role_id, topic, difficulty, question_text, ideal_answer) VALUES
(2, 'Bootstrap Grid System', 'Easy', 'What is the Bootstrap Grid System and how does it help developers build responsive web pages?', 'The Bootstrap Grid System is a responsive layout framework based on a twelve-column structure that helps developers organize webpage content efficiently across different screen sizes. It uses containers, rows, and columns to divide the available width into flexible sections that automatically adjust according to the device viewport. By applying predefined grid classes, developers can quickly create layouts that work consistently on desktops, tablets, and mobile phones without writing complex CSS media queries manually. This approach speeds up frontend development, improves maintainability, and ensures a consistent responsive design across multiple devices.'),
(2, 'HTML Forms', 'Easy', 'How do HTML forms collect user input and transmit it to a web server?', 'HTML forms are used to collect user input through various interactive controls like text boxes, checkboxes, radio buttons, and dropdown menus wrapped inside a form element. When a user fills out the form and clicks a submit button, the browser packages the input data into key-value pairs. This data is then transmitted to the web server specified in the form action attribute using either the GET or POST HTTP method. The GET method appends the parameters directly to the URL query string, making it suitable for searches, whereas the POST method includes the data in the HTTP request body, providing better security and capacity for sensitive or large submissions.'),
(2, 'HTML Tables', 'Easy', 'What are the key HTML elements used to structure a data table, and what is their purpose?', 'HTML tables are structured using a hierarchy of specific elements designed to present tabular data clearly and accessibly. The table element serves as the container for the entire grid. Inside, the table head element groups header content, while the table body element contains the main data rows, and the table foot element summarizes footer information. Individual rows are defined using the table row element, while standard data cells and header cells are created using table data and table header elements respectively. Using structural elements like caption for titles along with scope attributes on headers enhances accessibility and screen reader navigation across complex datasets.'),
(2, 'Local Storage', 'Easy', 'What is localStorage in web browsers and how is it used for client-side data persistence?', 'LocalStorage is a web storage API feature that allows JavaScript applications to store key-value pairs persistently within a user''s browser across multiple sessions and browser restarts. Unlike session storage or cookies, data saved in local storage does not expire automatically when the browser window is closed, remaining available until explicitly deleted via code or browser settings. It provides a simple synchronous interface with methods like set item to store data and get item to retrieve strings. This makes it ideal for saving user preferences, theme settings, or offline application states without burdening the server database, though it is limited to string data types and restricted by origin security policies.'),
(2, 'CSS Grid', 'Medium', 'How does CSS Grid Layout differ from Flexbox, and when should you use Grid?', 'CSS Grid Layout is a two-dimensional layout system designed for handling both columns and rows simultaneously, whereas Flexbox is primarily a one-dimensional system focused on laying out items in either a single row or a single column. CSS Grid excels when you need to define a complex structural layout or page skeleton where items must align across both horizontal and vertical axes independently. In contrast, Flexbox is ideal for distributing space and aligning items within smaller components or navigation bars. You should choose CSS Grid when building overall page layouts, card galleries, or dashboard interfaces requiring precise positioning across multi-dimensional grids without nesting excessive wrapper elements.'),
(2, 'Hoisting', 'Medium', 'What is hoisting in JavaScript, and how does it behave differently for var, let, and const declarations?', 'Hoisting is JavaScript''s default behavior where variable and function declarations are conceptually moved to the top of their enclosing scope during the compilation phase before code execution begins. When using var variables, they are hoisted and initialized with an undefined value, allowing them to be accessed before their actual line of declaration without throwing a syntax error. However, let and const declarations are also hoisted but are not initialized, placing them in a temporal dead zone from the start of the block until the declaration is evaluated. Accessing let or const variables before their declaration results in a reference error, enforcing safer and more predictable variable scoping practices.'),
(2, 'Closures', 'Medium', 'What is a closure in JavaScript, and what are its common practical use cases?', 'A closure is a powerful JavaScript feature where an inner function retains access to the outer function''s variables and scope even after the outer function has finished executing and returned. This happens because the inner function maintains a persistent reference to its lexical environment, allowing it to read and manipulate outer variables across multiple calls. Closures are commonly used for data privacy and encapsulation, enabling developers to create private variables that cannot be accessed directly from the global scope. They are also widely utilized in factory functions, callback handlers, and currying techniques where state needs to be maintained across asynchronous operations or event listeners without polluting the global namespace.'),
(2, 'Event Delegation', 'Medium', 'What is event delegation in JavaScript, and what are its main performance benefits?', 'Event delegation is a technique where a single event listener is attached to a parent element to manage events triggered on its current or future child elements, relying on the mechanism of event bubbling. When an event occurs on a child element, it bubbles up to the parent container, where the listener checks the event target property to identify which specific element was interacted with. The main performance benefit is that it significantly reduces the memory footprint and initialization overhead by avoiding the need to attach individual event listeners to hundreds of dynamically created or static child elements. It also ensures that newly added child elements automatically inherit event handling without requiring manual listener binding.'),
(2, 'Arrow Functions', 'Hard', 'How do arrow functions differ from traditional regular functions in JavaScript, particularly regarding scope and behavior?', 'Arrow functions provide a concise syntax for writing JavaScript functions, but they introduce important behavioral differences compared to traditional function declarations. The most significant distinction is that arrow functions do not have their own binding for the this keyword; instead, they lexically inherit this from the surrounding parent execution context. This behavior prevents unexpected context loss in asynchronous callbacks, event handlers, or array methods without requiring explicit variable binding or method chaining. Additionally, arrow functions cannot be used as constructors, do not have access to the arguments object, and cannot be hoisted, meaning they must be declared before they are called in the execution flow.'),
(2, 'Cookies', 'Hard', 'What are HTTP cookies, and how do flags like Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite enhance security?', 'HTTP cookies are small pieces of data sent from a web server and stored in the user''s browser, primarily used for session management, user tracking, and storing personalization preferences. Because cookies are automatically attached to subsequent HTTP requests, they are vulnerable to security threats like cross-site scripting and cross-site request forgery if not properly configured. Security flags mitigate these risks effectively by restricting cookie access and transmission channels. The HttpOnly flag prevents client-side scripts from accessing the cookie via document cookie, defending against cross-site scripting session theft. The Secure flag ensures cookies are only transmitted over encrypted HTTPS connections, while SameSite controls cross-origin request inclusion to prevent cross-site request forgery attacks.');
INSERT INTO questions (role_id, topic, difficulty, question_text, ideal_answer) VALUES
(2, 'HTTP Methods', 'Easy', 'What are HTTP methods, and what are the primary purposes of the GET and POST methods in web development?', 'HTTP methods are standard request verbs used by clients to communicate with web servers and specify the desired action to be performed on a resource. The GET method is used to retrieve data from a server without modifying any server-side state, making it safe and idempotent. When a browser sends a GET request, parameters are typically appended to the URL query string. In contrast, the POST method is used to submit data to be processed to a specified resource, often resulting in a change in server state or database updates. POST requests securely transmit data within the request body rather than the URL, making them suitable for sensitive user inputs and form submissions.'),
(2, 'CSS Units', 'Easy', 'Explain the difference between absolute and relative CSS units, and give examples of when to use each type.', 'CSS units determine the scale and sizing of elements on a web page and are categorized into absolute and relative types. Absolute units, such as pixels, inches, or points, maintain a fixed physical size regardless of screen dimensions or parent container properties. They are best suited for print style sheets or precise fixed-size borders where scaling is undesirable. Relative units, including percentages, ems, and rems, scale dynamically based on the parent element or the root font size. Frontend developers primarily prefer relative units for modern responsive design because they ensure layouts adapt smoothly across mobile devices, tablets, and desktop monitors, providing an accessible fluid user experience.'),
(2, 'Browser Developer Tools', 'Easy', 'How do web developers utilize browser developer tools to inspect and debug frontend applications effectively?', 'Browser developer tools are built-in suites of utilities integrated into modern web browsers that allow developers to inspect, modify, and debug the live Document Object Model and cascading style sheets in real time. Developers use the Elements panel to test layout tweaks and verify accessibility attributes instantly. The Console panel displays runtime errors, warnings, and allows executing arbitrary scripts for quick diagnostics. Additionally, the Network tab helps monitor HTTP requests, response headers, asset loading times, and payload sizes, enabling developers to identify performance bottlenecks, optimize resource delivery, and resolve broken API integrations swiftly during the web application development lifecycle.'),
(2, 'Session Storage', 'Easy', 'What is Session Storage in web development, and how does it differ from Local Storage?', 'Session storage is a web storage mechanism provided by modern browsers that allows frontend applications to store key-value data persistently within the user''s browser for the duration of a single page session. Data stored in session storage remains available across page reloads and navigation within the same tab or window, but is automatically and permanently cleared as soon as the user closes that specific browser tab. Unlike local storage, which persists indefinitely across browser restarts until explicitly deleted by scripts or user actions, session storage data is strictly isolated per tab and per origin, making it ideal for temporary state management during user workflows.'),
(2, 'JavaScript Promises', 'Medium', 'What is a JavaScript Promise, and how does it help manage asynchronous operations compared to traditional callback functions?', 'A JavaScript Promise is a proxy object representing the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. It acts as a cleaner alternative to traditional nested callback functions, which often lead to complex and unmaintainable code structures commonly known as callback hell. A promise exists in one of three states: pending, fulfilled, or rejected. Developers attach handler methods to process the resolved value or catch any encountered errors gracefully. By flattening asynchronous control flows and enabling predictable chaining syntax, promises significantly improve code readability, error handling consistency, and maintainability in modern web applications.'),
(2, 'async/await', 'Medium', 'Explain how async and await syntax simplifies working with asynchronous code in JavaScript.', 'The async and await keywords in JavaScript provide syntactic sugar built on top of standard promises, allowing developers to write asynchronous code that reads and behaves much like synchronous code. Declaring a function with the async keyword automatically ensures that it returns a promise, while the await keyword pauses the execution of the async function until the targeted promise settles and returns its result. This eliminates the need for explicit promise chains and cumbersome .then or .catch method calls. Consequently, developers can write straightforward try-catch blocks for error handling, resulting in cleaner, more intuitive code that is significantly easier to read and debug.'),
(2, 'CSS Specificity', 'Medium', 'What is CSS specificity, and how do browsers use it to resolve conflicting style rules on web pages?', 'CSS specificity is a weighting algorithm used by web browsers to determine which style declaration takes precedence when multiple conflicting rules target the same HTML element. Specificity is calculated based on the types of selectors used, ranked hierarchically from inline styles and IDs down to classes, attribute selectors, pseudo-classes, and finally element selectors or universal selectors. When rules collide, the browser applies the styles from the selector with the highest specificity score. If two competing rules have identical specificity scores, the browser resolves the conflict by applying the rule that appears last in the stylesheet according to the cascade origin and source order rules.'),
(2, 'REST APIs', 'Medium', 'What are the core architectural principles of REST APIs in modern web application development?', 'A REST API is an architectural style for designing networked applications based on stateless, client-server communication using standard HTTP protocols. Core principles include statelessness, meaning every client request must contain all necessary authentication and context data, allowing the server to process it independently without relying on stored session states. RESTful systems rely on a uniform interface where resources are identified by standard URLs and manipulated using standard HTTP verbs like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE. Additionally, REST architectures support cacheable responses to optimize network performance and promote layered system designs where clients interact seamlessly with intermediaries without knowing the backend server topology.'),
(2, 'CORS', 'Hard', 'What is Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS), and why is it essential for web browser security?', 'Cross-Origin Resource Sharing is a crucial security mechanism implemented by web browsers to enforce the same-origin policy, which restricts web pages from making asynchronous requests to a domain different from the one that served the web page. Without CORS, malicious websites could read sensitive user data from authorized external services by exploiting active user sessions. When a frontend application requests resources from a different origin, the browser sends an HTTP request containing origin headers. The target server responds with specific access control headers specifying permitted domains, methods, and credentials, allowing browsers to safely authorize or block cross-domain data exchanges while preventing unauthorized script access across disparate web origins.'),
(2, 'Event Loop', 'Hard', 'Explain how the JavaScript Event Loop manages execution context, call stack, and asynchronous callback execution in a single-threaded environment.', 'JavaScript is a single-threaded programming language, meaning it can execute only one piece of code at a time using a single call stack. The event loop is the architectural mechanism that allows JavaScript to perform non-blocking asynchronous operations by coordinating between the call stack, web APIs, task queue, and microtask queue. When asynchronous tasks such as timers or network requests finish, their callback functions are placed into queues. The event loop continuously monitors the call stack; when the stack is empty, it first processes all high-priority microtasks like promises, and then pulls tasks from the callback queue into the call stack for execution, ensuring smooth responsiveness.');
INSERT INTO questions (role_id, topic, difficulty, question_text, ideal_answer) VALUES
(2, 'Browser Rendering Process', 'Easy', 'What is the browser rendering process and how do browsers convert HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into pixels on the screen?', 'The browser rendering process refers to the steps a web browser takes to translate HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code into an interactive visual webpage on your screen. First, the browser parses the HTML document to build the Document Object Model tree and parses CSS to build the CSS Object Model tree. It then combines both trees into a render tree that determines the layout and styling of visible elements. Next, the browser paints the pixels onto the screen by drawing layers. Throughout this sequence, JavaScript can interrupt or modify the DOM, ensuring users experience dynamic updates while navigating modern websites seamlessly.'),
(2, 'Accessibility (ARIA)', 'Easy', 'What is Accessibility in web development and how do ARIA attributes improve screen reader compatibility?', 'Web accessibility ensures that websites and applications can be used by people with diverse disabilities, including visual, auditory, and motor impairments. Accessible design relies heavily on semantic HTML elements that natively communicate structure to assistive technologies like screen readers. When native elements are insufficient for complex custom widgets, developers use Accessible Rich Internet Applications attributes to provide missing semantic information. These attributes help describe roles, states, and properties so that visually impaired users receive clear context and interactive feedback when navigating through buttons, modals, sliders, and dynamic application menus across devices.'),
(2, 'SEO Basics', 'Easy', 'What are the fundamental principles of Search Engine Optimization and how do frontend developers impact website rankings?', 'Search Engine Optimization involves structuring and designing web pages so that search engines can easily crawl, index, and understand their content. Frontend developers play a crucial role by using proper semantic HTML tags, descriptive heading hierarchies, and meaningful alternative text for all images. Additionally, creating mobile responsive layouts, ensuring fast page load speeds, and implementing clean URL structures significantly boost search rankings. Search engines reward websites that provide excellent user experiences, semantic clarity, and optimal performance by placing them higher in organic search results, driving more targeted traffic to the platform.'),
(2, 'Lazy Loading', 'Easy', 'What is lazy loading and how does it improve initial page load performance for web applications?', 'Lazy loading is a web performance optimization technique that delays the loading of non-critical resources, such as images or videos, until the exact moment they are needed by the user. Instead of downloading all media files upfront during the initial page load, the browser loads only the content visible within the current viewport. As the user scrolls down the page, subsequent resources are fetched asynchronously just before they enter the screen. This approach significantly reduces initial data transfer size, lowers server bandwidth consumption, and dramatically accelerates page loading times, resulting in a much faster and smoother user experience.'),
(2, 'Reflow vs Repaint', 'Medium', 'What is the difference between a reflow and a repaint in browser rendering and how do they impact performance?', 'A reflow occurs when the browser calculates the geometric dimensions and positions of elements on a page, such as changing width, height, or padding, which can trigger cascading layout calculations across surrounding elements. A repaint happens when visual changes affect an element''s appearance without altering its layout geometry, such as modifying background colors or text color. Reflows are computationally expensive and heavily impact browser performance because they force the engine to recalculate layout coordinates for multiple nodes. Developers can optimize performance by batching DOM reads and writes or utilizing hardware-accelerated CSS properties to minimize unnecessary layout recalculations.'),
(2, 'Web Performance Optimization', 'Medium', 'What are key strategies for optimizing web application performance to ensure fast loading times and smooth user interaction?', 'Web performance optimization encompasses various techniques aimed at minimizing page load times and ensuring buttery-smooth runtime interactions. Key strategies include reducing overall bundle sizes through code splitting, compressing image assets, and leveraging browser caching to store static resources locally. Developers should also minimize critical rendering path latency by deferring non-essential scripts, utilizing content delivery networks to serve assets closer to users, and eliminating render-blocking stylesheets. Furthermore, optimizing DOM tree depths and avoiding memory leaks help maintain consistent frame rates, resulting in responsive web applications that keep users engaged and satisfied.'),
(2, 'CSS Animations', 'Medium', 'How do CSS animations work and what is the difference between using CSS transitions versus keyframe animations?', 'CSS animations allow developers to transition elements smoothly between different style states over time without relying on JavaScript or external plugins. CSS transitions are ideal for simple state changes triggered by user interactions, such as hovering over buttons or focusing input fields, by interpolating property values between a start state and an end state over a specified duration. In contrast, keyframe animations provide granular control over complex multi-step sequences by defining intermediate stages of an animation timeline using percentage markers. Both methods utilize hardware acceleration via the graphics processing unit when animating properties like transform and opacity.'),
(2, 'Progressive Enhancement', 'Medium', 'What is progressive enhancement and how does it differ from graceful degradation in web development?', 'Progressive enhancement is a web design strategy that emphasizes core webpage content and functionality first, serving a robust, accessible baseline experience to all users regardless of their browser capabilities or device limitations. Advanced features, such as complex JavaScript interactions, rich animations, and high-resolution graphics, are subsequently layered on top for modern browsers that support them. This approach contrasts with graceful degradation, which starts with a fully featured experience and strips away or simplifies functionality for older environments. Progressive enhancement guarantees universal accessibility, exceptional fault tolerance, and superior long-term maintainability across a diverse ecosystem of connected client devices.'),
(2, 'Service Workers', 'Hard', 'What is a service worker and how does it enable offline functionality and caching in progressive web apps?', 'A service worker is a programmable client-side proxy script that runs in the background separately from the main browser thread, intercepting and managing network requests made by a web application. By acting as a secure intermediary between the app and the network, service workers enable reliable offline functionality by caching essential static assets and API responses. When a user loses internet connectivity, the service worker can serve cached data directly, ensuring the application remains interactive and responsive. They also power background synchronization and push notifications, transforming standard web applications into immersive, app-like experiences comparable to native software installations.'),
(2, 'Web Workers', 'Hard', 'What is a Web Worker and how does it prevent blocking the main thread during heavy JavaScript computations?', 'A Web Worker is a background JavaScript mechanism that allows developers to run resource-intensive scripts and complex computations on separate threads away from the main browser thread. Since standard JavaScript execution is single-threaded, heavy calculations or data processing can freeze the user interface and cause unresponsive frame rates. Web Workers solve this problem by executing tasks asynchronously in isolated background environments and communicating with the main thread via asynchronous message passing. This keeps the user interface completely fluid, responsive, and interactive while computationally expensive operations run efficiently in the background without causing interface stuttering or browser warnings.');
INSERT INTO questions (role_id, topic, difficulty, question_text, ideal_answer) VALUES
(2, 'Browser Compatibility', 'Easy', 'What is browser compatibility in frontend development and why is it important for web applications?', 'Browser compatibility refers to the ability of a website or web application to function correctly and display consistently across different web browsers and their various versions. Since users access the web using diverse browsers like Chrome, Safari, Firefox, and Edge, developers must ensure that their HTML, CSS, and JavaScript behave uniformly everywhere. Differences in rendering engines can cause layout distortions, broken features, or script failures if compatibility is ignored. Ensuring broad compatibility involves using standardized coding practices, progressive enhancement, polyfills for missing features, and rigorous cross-browser testing, ultimately providing a seamless, reliable experience for every visitor regardless of their chosen software.'),
(2, 'Progressive Web Apps (overview)', 'Easy', 'What is a Progressive Web App (PWA) and what are its key characteristics?', 'A Progressive Web App is a type of application delivered through the web that utilizes modern web capabilities to deliver an app-like experience to users. PWAs combine the best of both web and mobile applications, offering reliability, speed, and engaging user experiences. Key characteristics include being reliable by working offline or on low-quality networks using service workers, fast loading times, and the ability to be installed directly on a device home screen without visiting an app store. They are responsive to any screen size and secure when served over HTTPS, bridging the gap between traditional websites and native mobile apps.'),
(2, 'Source Maps', 'Easy', 'What are source maps and how do they assist developers during debugging?', 'Source maps are files that map transformed, minified, or bundled production code back to its original source code. In modern frontend development, code is frequently compiled, optimized, and combined into single compressed files for performance, which makes debugging extremely difficult because error stack traces point to unreadable production lines. Source maps act as a bridge between the compressed output running in the browser and the original source files written by developers. When an error occurs, debugging tools use the source map to display the exact original file name and line number, greatly simplifying troubleshooting.'),
(2, 'CDN (Content Delivery Network)', 'Easy', 'What is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) and how does it improve website performance?', 'A Content Delivery Network is a globally distributed system of proxy servers and data centers designed to deliver web content to users with high availability and high performance. By caching static assets such as stylesheets, scripts, and images closer to physical user locations, a CDN drastically reduces network latency and server load. When a user requests a web page, the CDN serves the assets from the nearest edge server rather than routing the request back to the primary origin server. This geographical proximity ensures faster page loading times, improved user experience, and enhanced scalability during traffic spikes.'),
(2, 'Code Splitting', 'Medium', 'What is code splitting in modern frontend development and how does it optimize performance?', 'Code splitting is a optimization technique used by modern module bundlers to split your JavaScript code into smaller bundles which can then be loaded on demand or in parallel. Instead of downloading a massive single bundle containing the entire application code upon initial page load, the browser downloads only what is strictly necessary for the current view. Additional chunks are fetched dynamically as the user navigates through different features or pages. This significantly reduces initial load times, lowers bandwidth consumption, and improves overall application responsiveness, ensuring that users can interact with web pages much faster without unnecessary delays.'),
(2, 'XSS Prevention (Frontend Perspective)', 'Medium', 'What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and how can frontend developers prevent it?', 'Cross-Site Scripting is a security vulnerability where malicious scripts are injected into trusted websites viewed by other users, allowing attackers to steal session tokens, manipulate DOM content, or capture sensitive data. From a frontend perspective, prevention involves never trusting user input and properly sanitizing or escaping any dynamic data before rendering it in the user interface. Developers should avoid using dangerous inner HTML assignment methods when rendering untrusted content and instead rely on safe text binding techniques provided by modern frameworks. Implementing a robust Content Security Policy further restricts where scripts can be loaded and executed securely.'),
(2, 'Debouncing', 'Medium', 'What is debouncing in JavaScript and in which scenarios is it commonly applied?', 'Debouncing is a programming practice used to limit the rate at which a function can fire by ensuring that it does not execute again until a specific amount of time has passed since its last invocation. When rapid events like window resizing, scrolling, or keystrokes occur repeatedly, debouncing postpones function execution until the user stops triggering the event for the defined delay period. This prevents unnecessary processing, reduces server requests, and optimizes performance. It is commonly applied in search input autocomplete bars, where API calls are delayed until the user finishes typing their search query entirely.'),
(2, 'Throttling', 'Medium', 'What is throttling in JavaScript and how does it differ from debouncing?', 'Throttling is a technique used to control the execution frequency of a function by ensuring it is called at most once within a specified time interval, regardless of how many times the underlying event occurs. While debouncing waits for a pause in events before triggering, throttling guarantees regular execution at consistent intervals during continuous actions. Throttling is ideal for tracking continuous events like window scrolling, mouse movement, or window resizing, where you need periodic updates without overwhelming the browser with excessive calculations. This technique maintains smooth frame rates and responsive user interfaces during heavy interactive workloads.'),
(2, 'Tree Shaking', 'Hard', 'What is tree shaking in JavaScript bundlers and how does it reduce bundle size?', 'Tree shaking is a dead-code elimination technique used in modern JavaScript bundlers to remove unused exports from production builds. The term metaphorically describes shaking a tree to cause dead leaves to fall off. Tree shaking relies heavily on the static structure of ES modules, where import and export statements can be analyzed at compile time without running the code. The bundler traces the dependency graph from entry points and excludes any exported functions, variables, or modules that are never imported anywhere in the application, resulting in significantly smaller bundle sizes and faster download times for users.'),
(2, 'Memory Leaks in JavaScript', 'Hard', 'What causes memory leaks in single-page JavaScript applications and how can they be prevented?', 'Memory leaks in single-page JavaScript applications occur when objects that are no longer needed by the application are still referenced in memory, preventing the garbage collector from reclaiming that space. Common causes include detached DOM nodes left in global variables, forgotten event listeners, uncleaned timers or intervals, and lingering closures that retain scope references. Over time, accumulated leaks degrade application performance, cause sluggish interactions, and may crash the browser tab. Prevention requires proper cleanup during component unmounting, removing event listeners, clearing timers, and ensuring references to obsolete data structures are explicitly set to null.');
